# 11. Key Observations

| Area | Observation |
| --- | --- |
| Claim / Pass | Clearly understood and viewed as fair |
| Passing | Felt like opting out rather than accusing |
| Anonymous Veto | Useful, but anonymity was not fully trusted |
| Veto behaviour | Participant still wanted to know who triggered it |
| Spinner | Acceptable for low-value items but rejected for expensive costs |
| Review | Personal vs communal distinction was understood |
| Pizza slider | Viewed as realistic |
| Chore bounty | Generally acceptable but money values may create new arguments |
| Final bill | Participant could explain the final amount without help |
| Synchronous use | Preferred over receiving a later Splitwise notification |

# 12. Initial Design Implications

1. Strengthen the visual communication of Anonymous Veto so users trust that no identity is revealed.
2. Consider adding a maximum value or threshold for the Orphan Spinner.
3. Avoid using random allocation for high-cost expenses.
4. Keep item-level Claim / Pass because the participant strongly preferred it to equal splitting.
5. Keep a clear itemised final breakdown so users can understand where their payment comes from.
6. Consider whether fixed dollar values for chores could create new disagreements.
7. Preserve some space for face-to-face discussion, especially for expensive or disputed items.
